WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.120
Oh, wow.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.500
The Parkes radio telescope,
here in Australia,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.400
is most famous for receiving pictures
from the Apollo 11 moonwalk.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.780
But it's still in near-constant use
for astronomy and scientific research.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.400
Now, it's a maintenance day here today,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.340
which is why I'm allowed to be here
on the surface of the dish.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.100
And the dish is about to tilt over,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.080
nearly all the way
to touching the ground,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.820
and I'm going to be here
while it happens.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.840
I'm going for a ride!

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.020
But that would be a lot of
very slow, boring footage

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.240
of me just awkwardly standing
and sitting here

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.320
as my world tilts over.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.640
So while that's happening,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.080
let me show you how I got here.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.880
I'm going to be filming this awkwardly.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.800
We've got John here
who's going to show me around.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.180
We've got Jack behind the camera.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.140
Let's go see a telescope!

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.704
- Yeah. Let's go in, Tom, and see
what it's like inside it.

00:00:49.704 --> 00:00:52.980
- You can tell it was built
in the '60s and '70s, can't you?

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.300
Big asbestos warning.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.060
- This is the ground floor where we
have the air conditioning units

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.740
that help keep all our electronics cool.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.698
- And the astronomers, presumably!

00:00:59.698 --> 00:01:01.200
- And the astronomers too.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.880
Just behind Jack here,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.500
we have the most important
room in the building,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.000
which is the astronomer's kitchen.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.920
If they do visit,
they can stay on site.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.480
But over the last 10 years,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.580
we've switched to a
remote observing regime

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.980
where people can log in
over the internet to observe.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.920
So we have fewer and fewer
astronomers visiting now.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.120
But let's go on upstairs

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.160
and have a look in
the main control room.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:20.940
So, follow me on up.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:26.865
[warning siren]

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.240
- This is so weird, because...

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.060
the clouds are rotating!

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.020
I know the dish is rotating,
but I cannot see anything.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.660
- So, this is the old control room.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.840
The old control desk that was
recreated for the film, The Dish,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.400
used to be located about there.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.860
But in 1982, it was decommissioned
and replaced by a computerized desk

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.750
that used the PDP-11 computers.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.340
It doesn't crash very often,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.900
because it doesn't have
an operating system.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.940
It just stays up very reliably.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.160
And so we still use it to
drive the dish around

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.200
and to point the telescope too.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.940
If it's not broken,
we're not fixing it!

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.820
The radio waves coming down
from the stars,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.220
they're reflected off the
parabolic surface of the dish,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.840
and they're brought to the focus

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.520
where the radio waves
are concentrated.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.540
We can typically collect a
few terabytes of data a day,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.540
but with the new receivers that
we're commissioning later this year,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.800
we'll be looking at maybe
nine terabytes an hour.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.820
The telescope itself is an
extremely sensitive radio detector,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.580
because the energies that we're
collecting from the stars

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.900
are incredibly weak.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.400
If I was to release the feather

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.740
and let it slowly just drift
to the floor here like this,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.840
the energy that feather expended
when it struck the floor was more

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.860
than all the energy ever collected
by every radio telescope ever built,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.540
in terms of the astronomical data.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.080
And so that's why radio telescopes
have to be so large,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.960
because you want as large a
collecting area as possible.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.940
Any electronic devices will transmit
radio waves that could very easily

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.060
overwhelm the really feeble signals
we're trying to detect,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.230
such as mobile phones-

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.000
- Such as these wireless mics, by the way!

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.858
It's a maintenance day.
I got special permission for these!

00:03:04.858 --> 00:03:07.680
- That's right. A lot of this tech here
we've designed ourselves,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.620
but we also have other equipment
that we buy off the shelf,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.840
and then we have to
put them in shielded cabinets,

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.660
because they're not really designed
to be radio-quiet, so to speak.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.040
A mobile telephone on the moon's surface,
400,000 kilometers away,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.760
would be one of the brightest
radio sources in the sky for us.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.260
So if we can detect it
from the moon,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.840
we have no trouble detecting it
from the car park!

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.360
We refer to this as the
master control panel.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.540
If I was to turn this off here,
you can see it's on remote,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.040
it'll be on computer control
so that people off-site can log in,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.840
take control,
and move the telescope.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.100
But if I was to put it onto
local and master control,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.038
I'll be able to move it using these dials,
up and down, left and right.

00:03:47.038 --> 00:03:48.720
- Wait, the dials still work?

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.260
- Oh, yeah.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.740
[warning siren]

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.960
Oh, okay. Gravity is changing direction.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.340
I-- We got to walk this way.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.860
I guess we got to
stay horizontal here.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.780
I'm not actually sure what
horizontal is anymore.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:04.860
Am I still...?

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:05.880
No, I think I need
to go further.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.080
I'm not horizontal yet.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.460
I've got no reference frame.
I can't see the horizon.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.410
I can't see anything.
This is dizzying!

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.320
- Because we observe
remotely now, often,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.300
there's nobody in the tower
when we're observing.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.960
And so we need a way to make sure
the telescope's safe at all times.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.440
So we have this device called the
Telescope Protection System or TPS,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.320
which monitors hundreds of
data points on the telescope.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.360
And if it detects an issue
that might be a problem,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.100
it immediately alerts us

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.580
using SMS messages, emails,
telex, everything.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.100
And we can immediately descend
on the telescope,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.480
see what's wrong,
and fix it.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.100
And we have an anemometer,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.140
as soon as the wind reaches
a certain critical speed,

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.300
that's when we say,
the winds are too high,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.800
and we just stow the telescope.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.860
Because essentially, the telescope's
just a giant beach umbrella.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.540
- I mean, this thing's incredibly robust.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.600
It's held up to significant
wind and weather.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.120
- When it was commissioned
back in October 1961,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.580
it was only intended to
operate for 20 years.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.620
So we're now 41 years
over that.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.920
And the reason for it is because
we've continually upgraded the telescope.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.320
The most obvious upgrades have
been to the dish surface.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.720
- I'm not going to do anything,
but I... suddenly,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.400
I'm not walking on
the solid surface,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.420
I'm walking on the mesh
of the dish,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.780
and my brain is...
just about dealing with it?

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.120
- We're going up into the
dish surface here.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.940
So we're first going up
through the spiral-

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:21.720
- Spiral staircase!

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.400
-spiral staircase in the
central column.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.680
This central column is on a
separate foundation

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.580
to the rest of the building,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.620
because at the top of this column
is the master equatorial,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.960
the device that helps us point
the telescope accurately.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.120
So we don't want that connected to
the rest of the building

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.260
because the vibrations will cause
the pointings of the telescope to be out.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.700
So come on up.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.040
- So all the cables here are
transmission cables?

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.140
- That's right.
Yeah, they're bringing signals

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.180
down from the focus cabin,
and also,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.200
we send other commands up to operate
the equipment and power it, and so on.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.180
Okay.

00:05:57.479 --> 00:06:00.120
- I just looked over and there's
a horizon all of a sudden!

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.360
- We're now going to go through
the door down to the azimuth track.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.180
- Right. Okay. Oh, yeah. I just looked up.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.220
There's a telescope up there!

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.160
- Before we go all the way down,
you can see this is the twister room.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.760
The cables coming down from the
focus cabin come down here first.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.800
There are two counter-rotating rings.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.800
So as the dish rotates in azimuth,
they counter-rotate,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.540
so they take up the
slack and release it,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.358
so that the cables don't get
twisted and tangled or anything like that.

00:06:24.358 --> 00:06:25.880
- Oh, that's very clever.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.580
- So this level is what we
call the azimuth track,

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.080
and this is the actual
azimuth track.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.160
And if you look here,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.020
you can see it's
sitting on four rollers,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.100
but only two are driven.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.500
On this track, we move it
left and right like this.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.360
But you can see in
the center there,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.980
there's nothing actually
holding it down.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.060
It's just pivoted there.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.548
It's just the sheer weight of the structure
that keeps the telescope grounded.

00:06:46.558 --> 00:06:50.340
- Because everything from this level
on up weighs a thousand tonnes

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.920
or the equivalent of
three fully-laden jumbo jets.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.600
- Yeah, okay!

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:55.440
All right.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.260
- So when they built the
telescope back in the '50s,

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.800
they didn't have computers that could do
the coordinate conversions fast enough.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.700
So instead, Barnes Wallis,
a really famous British engineer,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.120
he came up with this device,
essentially said it's an optical telescope.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.640
And because that's the polar axis,

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.580
once you point the optical telescope
to the position you want on the sky,

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.900
you only have to move it at a
constant rate around the polar axis

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.820
so that you could then
follow the object

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:21.778
smoothly across the sky.

00:07:21.778 --> 00:07:27.540
- You're adjusting for the earth's rotation
by just rotating something

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:28.918
at the same angle
in the opposite direction.

00:07:28.918 --> 00:07:29.820
- In the opposite direction.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.400
And this was the secret
to the telescope's success,

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.260
we were able to point
the telescope

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.500
very, very accurately on the sky.
In fact, it was so successful

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.500
that all subsequent large,
single-dish antennas

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.860
employed a master equatorial
up until about the 1990s,

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.160
when the microprocessors
were small enough and fast enough

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.380
to do the coordinate conversions
on the fly.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.540
Before we go up,
let's have a little walk

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.580
around the super structure
just under the dish.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.780
So, here we go.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.080
- You're walking around here
a lot more casually than I am!

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:58.860
- Oh, okay!

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:01.980
You can see here,
this is a great view.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.580
You can see out towards the east
where we have the Hervey Ranges

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.560
that shield us from the
radio emissions

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.780
from the larger population centers
further east.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:10.380
- Right.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.720
- This is one of the reasons
why it was chosen.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:13.530
It's very radio-quiet.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:15.780
- All right.
So, next stop...

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:16.620
- Yeah.
- Surface.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.180
We'll go around.
We'll keep going around,

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.980
and then we'll end up
where we started.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.960
- If you just turn the camera
around that way for a moment,

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.060
that's where I was
standing a moment ago,

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.800
was in the middle of that dish.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.040
This ride I'm taking
isn't just a gimmick.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.660
It's called a "hayride"
sometimes,

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.660
but it turns out that's a term
that was invented by Americans

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.120
for a film that was shot here
more than 20 years ago.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.180
The folks at Parkes actually just
call this "riding the dish",

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.920
and they use it to get
heavy equipment

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.660
in and out of the central part.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.360
Because it's a lot easier
to let the dish do the lifting

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.700
instead of trying to actually
lug bulky gear

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.800
through all the corridors
and stairwells inside.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.320
Two hands on the railing...

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.020
and we're down!

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:56.700
Thank you very much, folks!

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.040
Thank you, Jack!

